/* SWE307 guided lessons. Sources: lecturer's chapters 1–7 and local NOTES.md.
 * The shared engine adds Practice & test yourself. Its check contract stores the
 * correct answer first and shuffles all answers when displaying each question.
 */
(() => {
  const flow = (t, items) => ({ type: 'flow', t, items: items.map(([a,b], i) => ['', a, b, 'c' + i % 6]) });
  const compare = (t, cols) => ({ type: 'compare', t, cols: cols.map(([t,...pts],i) => ({t,pts,c:'c'+i})) });
  const anim = name => ({type:'anim',name:'swe-'+name});
  const table = (heads, rows) => '<table class="t"><tr>'+heads.map(x=>'<th>'+x+'</th>').join('')+'</tr>'+rows.map(r=>'<tr>'+r.map(x=>'<td>'+x+'</td>').join('')+'</tr>').join('')+'</table>';
  const warn = text => '<div class="callout warn">'+text+'</div>';
  const lab = (href,...todo) => ({href,todo});
  const step = (t,key,fig,html,q,o,why,lab) => ({t,key,fig,html,check:{q,o,why},...(lab?{lab}:{})});
  const videos = ids => '<p>Watch the chapter explainers: '+ids.map(([id,title])=>'<a href="videos.html#'+id+'">'+title+'</a>').join(' · ')+'.</p>';
  window.GUIDE = {
    1: {
      goal: 'explain what an information system contains, separate analysis from design, assign stakeholder responsibilities, and judge software quality against specifications and user needs.',
      time: '60–75 min',
      steps: [
        step('Engineering turns a real need into a tested solution', 'Software engineering uses a <b>systematic, disciplined, quantifiable</b> approach throughout the life of software.',
          flow('Engineering work', [['Need','A practical problem'],['Analyze and design','Choose a workable solution'],['Build and test','Check the result']]),
          `<p>Picture the bridge mentioned in the slides. Making it work requires science and mathematics, but also decisions about safety, cost and practical limits.</p><p>A software engineer applies the same disciplined approach to software. Writing code is one activity among analysis, design, testing, deployment and maintenance.</p><p>Quantifiable means that we can measure aspects of the work. A team needs evidence about its product, rather than a feeling that the code looks finished.</p>`+videos([['whathow','What and how']]),
          'A team has written the code but has not tested or documented it. Why is its engineering work incomplete?', ['Software engineering also covers testing, documentation and later operation','Engineering ends when code compiles','Only the owner can test software'], 'The slides describe a life of activities, including development, operation and maintenance.'),
        step('A software product needs people and a process', 'The four Ps connect <b>people</b>, <b>product</b>, <b>project</b> and <b>process</b>.',
          compare('Four views of the same work', [['People','Stakeholders contribute and are affected'],['Product','Software plus associated documents'],['Project','Connected work within time and budget'],['Process','Order, frequency and deliverables']]),
          '<p>Think about receiving a new application. You need the software, but you may also need instructions and test records. These belong to the product.</p><p>The project is the connected work that produces that result. People carry out the work, while the process tells them how the activities fit together.</p><p>A process model such as waterfall or agile is a particular way to organize that framework. The project still needs a goal, a budget and a completion time.</p>',
          'The team changes the order and frequency of reviews. Which part of the four Ps is being changed directly?', ['Process','Product size','System owner'], 'Order, frequency and deliverables are defined by the process.'),
        step('An information system is more than its computer', '<b>Hardware, software, data, processes and people</b> must work together to turn data into useful information.',
          flow('Information work', [['Collect','People enter data'],['Process and store','Software runs on hardware'],['Distribute','Information supports work']]),
          '<p>Imagine information being entered, stored and later used by another person. The computer alone cannot explain the whole activity.</p><p>Hardware provides the physical layer. Software controls it. Data is the raw material, and the business process explains the work people perform to get a result.</p><p>The slides call a system a set of interacting elements with a common purpose. An information system has the purpose of collecting, processing, storing and distributing information.</p>',
          'A computer works correctly, but users cannot complete the business task. What should the analyst examine?', ['People and business processes as well as the technology','Only the processor speed','Only the number of program files'], 'The five components work together; a working machine does not guarantee a working information system.'),
        step('Analysis says WHAT, design says HOW', '<b>Analysis</b> identifies what the system must do; <b>design</b> specifies how to meet those requirements.',
          compare('Two questions', [['Analysis','Collect facts','Identify needs and problems','Define WHAT'],['Design','Choose modules and interfaces','Plan the technical solution','Define HOW']]),
          '<p>Before building anything, ask what work the users need to complete. Collect facts and break the existing system into parts to understand the problem.</p><p>That is analysis. Design then plans the components and modules that can satisfy those needs.</p><p>Keep the two questions separate when reading an exam statement. A statement about a needed result belongs to analysis. A choice about technical construction belongs to design.</p>',
          'The team agrees on the required result, then chooses a database and screen structure. What is the second decision doing?', ['Designing how to satisfy the requirement','Discovering whether users exist','Replacing the project budget'], 'Technical choices translate the required WHAT into a proposed HOW.'),
        step('Success balances user needs, time and money', '<b>Project success</b> requires meeting user needs, delivering on time and staying within budget.',
          compare('Three success tests', [['Needs','Does the system support the users?'],['Time','Was it delivered on time?'],['Budget','Was the agreed cost respected?']]),
          '<p>A delivery date is easy to see on a calendar. Whether the delivered system helps its users needs a separate check.</p><p>The slides require all three measures: user needs, time and budget. Completing code early does not compensate for solving the wrong problem.</p><p>The owner funds the system and sets its vision and priorities. The project manager plans, monitors and controls schedule, budget, deliverables and quality.</p>',
          'A system arrives on time and within budget but cannot do the users’ required work. Is it successful by the slides’ measures?', ['No, meeting user needs is also required','Yes, two out of three is enough','Yes, delivery is the only measure'], 'The three measures apply together, rather than as alternative ways to pass.'),
        step('The analyst connects a business need to the builders', 'A <b>systems analyst</b> studies problems, opportunities and directives before designers and builders construct the solution.',
          flow('From need to delivery', [['Analyst','Understands business needs'],['Designer','Plans the technical solution'],['Builder','Constructs from the design'],['Tester and user','Check and use the result']]),
          '<p>A complaint is one reason to study a system. The analyst also looks for opportunities to improve work even without complaints, and directives that require change.</p><p>The analyst must understand who makes decisions and who needs information. The designer turns those findings into a technical solution, and the builder constructs its parts.</p><p>The tester detects and tracks defects. Users enter, validate, store and exchange information during their normal work. A stakeholder can therefore be affected by a system without writing any code.</p>',
          'No one complains, but an analyst sees a chance to improve a business activity. How should that reason for change be treated?', ['As an opportunity worth investigating','As a defect already proven in code','As a directive that must be a legal act'], 'An opportunity can justify improvement despite the absence of complaints.'),
        step('The life cycle hands work from one phase to the next', 'The <b>SDLC</b> connects planning, analysis, design, implementation and maintenance.', anim('sdlc'),
          '<p>Think of each phase handing its result to the next person. Planning organizes the work. Analysis defines what to build, and design describes how to build it.</p><p>Chapter 1 groups programming, testing and installation under implementation. Maintenance then resolves problems and adapts the released software to new requirements.</p><p>Models help communicate those decisions. A use case can show user goals, while class, activity, sequence and state diagrams show other views of the same system.</p>',
          'A released system must adapt to a new user requirement. Which chapter 1 phase addresses that work?', ['Maintenance','Initial planning only','Design with no implementation'], 'Maintenance includes adapting software after delivery, as well as fixing problems.'),
        step('Quality needs both the right construction and the right result', '<b>Verification</b> checks construction against specifications; <b>validation</b> checks the result against user requirements.',
          compare('Two quality questions', [['Verification','Are we building the product right?','Reviews and inspections'],['Validation','Are we building the right product?','Does it meet specified user needs?']]),
          '<p>A program can follow a design correctly while the design misses what users need. That is why one quality question is insufficient.</p><p>A defect is a deviation from requirements. Defect density compares defect count with software size. Mean time to failure measures elapsed time between crashes.</p><p>Finding defects early reduces repair cost. The slides place verification before validation and use inspections and reviews as verification activities, with software testing as validation.</p>'+warn('Do not swap the exam words: verification asks “built right”; validation asks “right product” for the users.'),
          'A code review confirms the implementation matches the design. What has this established?', ['Verification evidence, but not yet proof that user needs are met','That validation is unnecessary','That the system has no possible defects'], 'Matching a design answers the construction question. User requirements still need to be checked.')
      ]
    },
    2: {
      goal: 'follow SDLC deliverables from plan to maintenance, select the right testing level, and explain when sequential work or repeated feedback helps a project.', time:'70–90 min',
      steps: [
        step('The SDLC is a relay of deliverables', 'Each <b>SDLC phase</b> produces a result that supports the next activity.', anim('waterfall'),
          '<p>Picture the team passing a plan, then a requirements document, then a design to the people who need them.</p><p>Chapter 2 separates planning, requirements analysis, software design, development, testing, deployment and maintenance. The phase names tell you what question is being answered.</p><p>A deliverable is the visible result of that work. Following the documents helps you avoid treating all phases as “coding”.</p>'+videos([['sdlc','SDLC phases'],['models','Process models']]),
          'Which order preserves the reasoning behind the code?', ['Agree on requirements, design a solution, then develop it','Develop everything, then ask what users need','Deploy first, then decide the project scope'], 'Requirements define the target; design supplies the construction plan; development implements it.'),
        step('Planning puts boundaries around the work', 'The <b>SPMP</b> records scope, resources, schedule and cost as an estimate that can be revised.',
          flow('Build a project plan', [['Problem statement','Problems, opportunities, directives'],['Constraints','Limits the team must respect'],['SPMP','Scope, feasibility, work and estimates']]),
          '<p>The slides give a concrete deadline: the system must operate by April 15. They also give a $350,000 cost limit, a web requirement and a 15-day billing policy.</p><p>These constraints restrict the choices the team can make. Planning also identifies customers, major functionality, scope and initial feasibility.</p><p>The Software Project Management Plan is a rough estimate. It must be adapted as the team learns more during the project.</p>',
          'New information changes the effort estimate. What should happen to the SPMP?', ['Revise the plan to reflect the new information','Keep the first estimate because plans cannot change','Replace the requirements with source code'], 'The planning slides explicitly say the rough plan needs modification throughout the project.'),
        step('Requirements make user expectations explicit', 'The <b>SRS</b> describes what users and the business need from the application.',
          flow('Discover requirements', [['Find root causes','Investigate the problem'],['Ask actors','Gather evidence of needs'],['Write SRS','Use scenarios, stories and models']]),
          '<p>Before choosing modules, discover what people expect the system to accomplish. An actor can be a person or another system that exchanges information with it.</p><p>The analyst collects evidence through sampling, observation, interviews and other fact-finding methods. Root causes can be explored with a fishbone diagram.</p><p>The Software Requirements Specification records the resulting requirements. Use case scenarios and user stories help describe the work from the user’s view.</p>',
          'The analyst is writing how actors interact to complete a business event. What is that text useful for?', ['Explaining required behavior in a use case scenario','Measuring source-code lines','Replacing all user discussions'], 'Scenarios explain the event and the actor–system interactions needed to accomplish it.'),
        step('Design moves from the whole system to individual parts', 'The <b>SDD</b> explains architecture and detailed design for satisfying the requirements.',
          compare('Two design levels', [['Architecture','Break software into modules','Define interfaces between modules'],['Detailed design','Work out each module','Specify algorithms and data structures']]),
          '<p>Once the required work is understood, the team needs a construction plan. First decide how the software will be divided into subsystems.</p><p>Architecture describes those modules and their interfaces. Detailed design works through the inside of each part, including algorithms and data structures.</p><p>The Software Design Document records these decisions. UML diagrams, E-R diagrams, data flow diagrams and decision tables can communicate different parts of the design.</p>',
          'Choosing how modules communicate belongs most directly to which design level?', ['Software architecture','Post-deployment training','Requirements discovery only'], 'Architecture specifies the breakdown into modules and the interfaces between them.'),
        step('Development builds parts and connects them', '<b>Programming</b> translates design into code; <b>integration</b> assembles the parts.',
          flow('Development output', [['Design','Module and interface specifications'],['Program','Implement individual parts'],['Integrate','Assemble code ready for testing']]),
          '<p>A pile of completed modules is not yet an assembled application. The interfaces between those parts also have to work.</p><p>Builders implement the source code and interfaces from the design, then integrate the software parts.</p><p>The phase output is program code ready to be tested for correctness. Completion of development does not itself establish that the system meets user requirements.</p>',
          'All modules compile alone, but the application fails when they exchange data. What work needs attention?', ['Integration and interfaces','Only the project vision','Only user training'], 'Integration assembles parts, so failures between them require examination of their interfaces.'),
        step('Different tests answer different questions', '<b>Unit, integration, regression and system tests</b> examine different scopes and risks.',
          compare('What is under test?', [['Unit','One module'],['Integration','Interfaces between modules'],['Regression','Existing features after a change'],['System','The complete system against requirements']]),
          '<p>Testing one module cannot establish that all modules work together. Start by naming the scope of the question.</p>'+table(['Test','Question'],[['Unit','Does this individual module work?'],['Integration','Do the assembled modules interface properly?'],['Regression','Did a change damage existing behavior?'],['System','Does the whole system meet requirements?']])+'<p>Beta testing puts nearly finished software in users’ hands. Acceptance testing is conducted by the project owner after beta testing to check release criteria.</p><p>A changed feature may pass its own test while breaking an old feature. That is the reason for regression testing.</p>',
          'A change fixes a bug. The team now checks that earlier features still work. Why?', ['To detect regressions caused by the change','To repeat initial feasibility analysis','To avoid all future system tests'], 'Regression testing looks for adverse effects on existing behavior after a change.'),
        step('Deployment prepares real users; maintenance supports them', '<b>Deployment</b> makes software operational; <b>maintenance</b> repairs and improves released software.',
          flow('Going live and continuing', [['Deploy','Install, convert data and train users'],['Review','Record experience and update documents'],['Maintain','Fix defects and handle enhancements']]),
          '<p>Going live involves people and data as well as installing software. Users need the deployment schedule, changed responsibilities and a way to obtain support.</p><p>Deployment produces the delivered system, release descriptions and trained users. A review records lessons, and documents must reflect the production version.</p><p>Maintenance handles defects found in ordinary use and requests for improvement after release.</p>'+warn('Slide 33 calls deployment the “final” SDLC phase, but slides 4 and 39–40 explicitly place maintenance after it. State the context rather than silently deleting maintenance.'),
          'Users request a performance improvement after release. Where does it fit in the chapter’s full phase list?', ['Maintenance','Only the first planning phase','Before requirements can exist'], 'The maintenance slides include changes to performance and other attributes after release.'),
        step('Waterfall makes each phase wait for its predecessor', '<b>Waterfall</b> organizes work sequentially, with phase outputs becoming the next phase’s inputs.', anim('waterfall'),
          '<p>Imagine finishing all requirements before starting all design. The strict waterfall description runs each phase once, in sequence.</p><p>This is simple to manage because each phase has a defined deliverable. The slides say it suits smaller projects whose requirements are well understood.</p><p>Uncertain requirements make this harder. Important problems may appear only during late testing, and people can wait idle for an earlier phase. The slides therefore also show feedback between adjacent phases in practice.</p>',
          'Why is strict waterfall risky when users are unsure what they need?', ['Major requirement problems may be discovered late','It forces feedback after every small tested feature','It has no deliverables'], 'Doing all requirements and development before full testing can postpone discovery of major problems.'),
        step('Iterations test a part and learn before building more', '<b>Iterative development</b> repeats analysis, design, development and testing to refine the system.', anim('iterative'),
          '<p>Instead of waiting for the entire application, develop and test a part. Show that result, collect feedback and use it in the next cycle.</p><p>Each iteration includes some analysis, design, development and testing. The result refines requirements as well as code.</p><p>The slides allow separate teams to develop iterations in parallel. Repeated cycles do not automatically mean that all work must be serial.</p>',
          'What makes an iteration useful for reducing uncertainty?', ['A tested result provides feedback for the next cycle','It postpones testing until every cycle finishes','It prevents requirements from being refined'], 'The feedback from a working part informs later requirements, design and implementation.'),
        step('Agile uses frequent working results to respond to change', '<b>Agile</b> emphasizes working software, customer collaboration and responding to change.',
          compare('Agile priorities in the slides', [['People and feedback','Skilled individuals and interactions','Customer collaboration'],['Working increments','Working software','Responding to change']]),
          '<p>A customer can react more clearly to a working feature than to an unseen future system. Agile makes these feedback opportunities frequent.</p><p>The slides describe small teams, regular customer meetings, stories, pair programming and continuous testing. Refactoring changes internal structure while keeping external behavior.</p><p>Documentation is produced as needed. The slides also warn that documentation output can be weak and that very small or very large projects can be problematic.</p>',
          'A team improves internal code structure while preserving what users observe. How does this support the agile approach described?', ['It is refactoring that keeps the code workable as it evolves','It is deployment because behavior changes','It removes the need for customer feedback'], 'Refactoring improves existing structure without changing external behavior.')
      ]
    },
    3: {
      goal: 'explain XP and Scrum routines, choose an organization structure, calculate communication channels, and distinguish software size from project cost.', time:'75–90 min',
      steps: [
        step('A short cycle turns feedback into the next version', '<b>Iterative and agile development</b> use repeated working results to refine the product.', anim('iterative'),
          '<p>Think about showing the customer one running feature. The conversation can now be about something visible, rather than a guess about the final system.</p><p>Each iteration includes analysis, design, development and testing. Agile adds frequent customer contact, small teams and a focus on working code.</p><p>Stories describe needs. Pair programming and continual testing support implementation. Documentation is prepared as needed, with the slide warning that its quality can suffer.</p>'+videos([['xpscrum','XP and Scrum'],['teams','Teams and channels']]),
          'After an iteration, a customer clarifies a requirement. What should the next cycle do?', ['Use the feedback to refine the system','Ignore it because requirements never change','Delay all tests until the final release'], 'Successive iterations refine requirements, design, implementation and tests.'),
        step('XP puts a tested week inside a release plan', 'In the slides’ <b>XP</b> schedule, a weekly cycle is an iteration and a quarterly cycle is a release.',
          flow('XP planning rhythm', [['Quarterly cycle','Keep the overall release in view'],['Start of week','Reflect and select customer stories'],['End of week','Have running tested features']]),
          '<p>The slides ask the XP team to meet on the first day of the week. Customers choose the stories they want delivered, and the team decides how to approach them.</p><p>The weekly goal is running, tested features. The quarterly cycle keeps those small commitments connected to the overall release.</p><p>Communication, simplicity, feedback, respect and courage are the XP values. Courage includes being truthful about estimates and progress.</p>',
          'A weekly XP cycle ends with design notes but no running tested feature. What is missing?', ['The stated end-of-week result','A mandatory year-long iteration','A ban on customer choices'], 'The weekly cycle aims to deliver running tested features, within the larger release context.'),
        step('Test first, watch it fail, then make it pass', '<b>Test-driven development</b> starts with a failing automated test before the implementation.', anim('tdd'),
          '<p>Suppose a required behavior has no implementation yet. First write an automated test describing that behavior.</p><ol><li>Run the test and observe failure.</li><li>Develop code that makes the test pass.</li><li>Run it again and continue with the next test.</li></ol><p>Refactoring can improve the internal structure while preserving behavior. It does not mean adding a different user-visible feature.</p><p>The XP rules also ask for tests when bugs are found and for all unit tests to pass before release.</p>',
          'Why run the new test before implementing the behavior?', ['To observe the expected failure before making it pass','To prove the behavior is already complete','To replace the need to run it after coding'], 'The slide sequence explicitly includes running the failing test before implementation.'),
        step('XP keeps shared code understandable and integrated', '<b>Pair programming, collective ownership and continuous integration</b> help the whole team maintain shared code.',
          compare('Shared development practices', [['Pair programming','Driver writes','Navigator reviews'],['Collective ownership','Any developer can change needed code'],['Continuous integration','Merge many times a day','Find integration issues early']]),
          '<p>Two programmers at one workstation can review code as it is written. The driver types while the navigator watches and reviews.</p><p>Collective ownership lets a developer change any needed section. A coding standard and a simple design help other people understand those changes.</p><p>Frequent integration exposes conflicts between parts early. Customer availability and acceptance tests keep the shared work connected to user expectations.</p>'+warn('Slide 26 names sustainable pace and a 40-hour week, but also mentions extreme hours near a release. Report that tension honestly; the managing rules again say to set a sustainable pace.'),
          'Why merge the team’s work many times a day in XP?', ['To notice integration issues early','To postpone testing until release','To give each file a permanent sole owner'], 'Continuous integration brings separately developed work together frequently so problems surface sooner.'),
        step('Scrum turns backlog items into a working increment', 'The slides organize <b>Scrum</b> around a backlog, 30-day sprints and a daily meeting of at most 15 minutes.',
          flow('Scrum in this chapter', [['Backlog','Customer wants as user stories'],['Sprint','Work on a self-contained area'],['Daily scrum','Brief progress coordination'],['Increment','Working result']]),
          '<p>Start with the list of customer wants and needs. The slides call it the backlog, with each item expressed as a user story.</p><p>The chapter describes 30-day sprints. A scrum master conducts daily meetings, measures progress and makes decisions; the daily scrum should take no more than 15 minutes.</p><p>The team needs the skills to deliver product increments. The team-size slide says developers usually number two to five.</p>'+warn('The sources disagree on team size: slide 32 says 6–9, while slide 45 says 3–9. Keep both with their context instead of inventing one consistent range.'),
          'A daily scrum becomes a two-hour meeting. Which stated Scrum limit has been exceeded?', ['The daily meeting should last at most 15 minutes','The sprint must last 15 minutes','The backlog may contain only one story'], 'The 15-minute limit applies to the daily scrum, while the chapter describes 30-day sprints.'),
        step('Organization decides how people belong to projects', '<b>Project, functional and matrix</b> structures trade dedicated focus against sharing expertise.', anim('organization'),
          '<p>Imagine needing a specialist on a project. Is the specialist dedicated to this project, kept in a functional group, or loaned from that group?</p>'+table(['Structure','Arrangement','Tradeoff from slides'],[['Project','Staff dedicated to one project','Predictability improves; isolation reduces reuse'],['Function','Groups organized by expertise','Clear responsibility; moving staff can delay work'],['Matrix','Functional staff loaned to projects','Efficient use of resources']])+'<p>The structure influences coordination and scheduling. It does not tell you which process model the team uses.</p><p>In the sorter, explain where the employee belongs before choosing the structure.</p>',
          'An engineer belongs to a functional group but is loaned to projects for specialist work. Why is this matrix organization?', ['It combines functional membership with project assignments','It dedicates all employees permanently to one project','It has no functional groups'], 'The combination of a functional home and temporary project work defines the slide’s matrix structure.',
          lab('ch3/organization.html','Sort the dedicated-project staffing case.','Sort the loaned specialist case as matrix and explain the resource-sharing advantage.')),
        step('Adding people can increase delay on a failing project', '<b>Brooks’s law</b> warns that adding people to a failing project can make matters worse.',
          compare('More staff creates two effects', [['Possible benefit','Work can be divided into more parts'],['Coordination cost','More communication','More opportunity for errors']]),
          '<p>A late project can tempt a manager to add people immediately. More people may divide the work, but they must also coordinate.</p><p>The slides call communication time-consuming and error-prone in large teams. This disadvantage may outweigh the benefit of dividing labor.</p><p>Brooks’s warning is therefore a reason to examine the coordination problem before assuming that a larger team will recover the schedule.</p>'+warn('Do not divide remaining work by the new headcount and assume duration falls in the same proportion. The chapter explicitly warns against that assumption.'),
          'A failing project doubles its team. Which prediction is justified by the slides?', ['Coordination may outweigh the benefit and worsen the delay','Completion time must be halved','Communication work stays constant'], 'More staff introduces more coordination; a failing project is not automatically rescued by headcount.'),
        step('Eight people create 28 pairwise communication channels', 'For <b>N people</b>, the number of pairwise channels is <b>N(N−1)/2</b>.', anim('channels'),
          '<p>Draw a line for each pair of people who may need to communicate. A line from A to B is the same pair as B to A.</p><ol><li>For eight people, each person has seven possible partners: 8 × 7 = 56.</li><li>Every pair was counted twice, so divide by two.</li><li>The result is 28 channels.</li></ol><p>The slides also give 3 people → 3, 4 → 6 and 5 → 10. Channel count grows faster than headcount.</p><p>Use the lab picture to check the formula against actual lines.</p>',
          'A team grows from four people to five. How many channels are added?', ['4 channels','1 channel','5 channels'], 'Four people have 6 channels and five have 10, so the increase is 4.',
          lab('ch3/channels.html','Set N = 8 and count the lines: 28.','Change N from 4 to 5; compare 6 channels with 10.')),
        step('A budget separates building costs from running costs', '<b>Development costs</b> occur during creation; <b>operating costs</b> recur throughout system use.',
          compare('Cost categories', [['Development','One-time project costs','People, equipment and training'],['Operating: fixed','Regular intervals and fairly fixed rates','License renewal'],['Operating: variable','Changes with usage','Repair or replacement']]),
          '<p>The project does not stop costing money when its first version is delivered. A budget must consider both creation and later operation.</p><p>The slides classify development expenses as one-time costs. Operating expenses recur, with fixed costs such as license renewal and variable costs such as repairs.</p><p>Schedule estimates also depend on people, project size and complexity. Estimation starts in planning and continues after coding begins.</p>',
          'A license is renewed at regular intervals at a fairly fixed rate. Where does it belong?', ['Fixed operating cost','Only initial development effort','A communication channel'], 'The slides use license renewal as their example of a recurring fixed operating cost.'),
        step('Code length needs a counting rule and a language', '<b>LOC</b> measures physical code size; <b>FP</b> measures functionality.',
          compare('Two size views', [['LOC','Depends on implementation technology','Requires consistent counting rules'],['FP','Based on user-level functionality','Used before code is complete']]),
          '<p>Two programs can provide similar functionality with different amounts of code. The language affects the line count.</p><p>Before comparing LOC, agree whether blank lines, comments or only executable statements count. Otherwise the comparison can be misleading.</p><p>KLOC means a thousand lines: 22,500 LOC = 22.5 KLOC. Early in a project, historical data from similar completed projects helps because the new code does not yet exist.</p>',
          'Two teams report LOC using different rules for comments and blank lines. What is needed before comparing size?', ['A shared counting rule','Assume every line count is directly comparable','Convert both reports to number of people'], 'The slides stress defining counting rules so LOC comparisons are valid.')
      ]
    },
    4: {
      goal: 'count and rank function points, turn a 500-FP size into effort and duration estimates, and calculate the critical path of the eight-task project.', time:'110–140 min',
      steps: [
        step('Count the weather app from the user’s side', '<b>Function point analysis</b> counts user-level functionality: weather has <b>3 EI, 1 EO and 1 EQ</b>.',
          compare('Weather transactions', [['EI: 3','Add','Change','Delete'],['EO: 1','Report includes calculated averages'],['EQ: 1','Scroll retrieves existing entries']]),
          '<p>The weather application captures daily temperature and rainfall by city and state. It has one screen, one database and one report.</p><p>FPA measures specified functionality independently of implementation technology. The screen being one function in the example does not mean it contributes only one FP.</p><p>Count add, change and delete separately: each brings data across the application boundary into the system, giving three external inputs.</p><p>The report sends derived monthly averages outward, contributing one external output. The scroll-bar retrieval of earlier entries contributes one external inquiry.</p><p>The slides also say that retrieving data from another application and adding it locally increments both EQ and EI. Read the whole operation before assigning counts.</p>'+warn('EO includes derived data; EQ retrieves data. Do not classify the calculated averages as a plain inquiry.')+videos([['fpa','Function point analysis'],['estimate','Effort estimation'],['critical','Critical path']]),
          'The report computes monthly average rainfall. Why does the example count it as EO?', ['It sends derived data outward','All database access is automatically EI','It is a reference file owned elsewhere'], 'The average is calculated output, which is the distinguishing feature of this EO.'),
        step('Count stored groups by which application owns them', 'The weather example has <b>1 ILF</b> and <b>0 EIF</b>.',
          compare('Data function ownership', [['ILF','Related data inside the application boundary'],['EIF','Reference data used here','An ILF maintained by another application']]),
          '<p>The weather database stores city, state, date, temperature and rainfall. The example treats these related data as one internal logical file.</p><p>An external interface file would be reference data maintained by another application. This weather application has none, so its EIF count is zero.</p><p>Five fields do not mean five files. Counting logical data groups and counting their fields are different operations.</p>',
          'The weather database stores five fields as one related group. How many ILFs does the slide count?', ['1','5','0'], 'The group is one ILF; five is its DET count later in the ranking calculation.'),
        step('Rank transactions by DET and FTR', '<b>DET</b> counts recognizable non-repeated fields; <b>FTR</b> counts files referenced or updated.',
          compare('Weather transaction rankings', [['Each EI','6 DET, 1 FTR','Low'],['EO','7 DET, 0 FTR in the slide','Low'],['EQ','6 DET, 1 FTR','Low']]),
          '<p>For an EI, the slides count five input fields plus one multiple action key. That makes six DETs, with one referenced file.</p><p>Find the FTR row and DET column in the appropriate table. The EI uses different DET bands from EO and EQ.</p>'+table(['EI FTR / DET','1–4','5–15','16+'],[['0–1','Low','Low','Average'],['2','Low','Average','High'],['3+','Average','High','High']])+table(['EO or EQ FTR / DET','1–5','6–19','20+'],[['0–1','Low','Low','Average'],['2–3','Low','Average','High'],['4+','Average','High','High']])+'<p>The report has seven DETs, including two averages; the example records zero FTRs. The inquiry has six DETs and one FTR. Both rank low in their slide tables.</p>'+warn('Do not use the EI column boundaries for EO/EQ, and do not use RET to rank a transaction.'),
          'An EI has 2 FTRs and 6 DETs. Which cell gives its rank?', ['Average','Low','High'], 'Use the EI row for 2 FTRs and the 5–15 DET column: Average.'),
        step('Rank stored data by DET and RET', '<b>ILF and EIF</b> use record subgroups (<b>RET</b>) and fields (<b>DET</b>) for complexity.',
          compare('Which ranking inputs?', [['Transactions','FTR + DET'],['Data functions','RET + DET','Weather ILF: 1 RET, 5 DET → Low']]),
          '<p>Now look inside the weather data group. It has one subgroup of related data, so it has one RET, and its five fields give five DETs.</p><p>The same complexity table is used for ILF and EIF. Weather has no EIF to rank.</p>'+table(['RET / DET','1–19','20–50','51+'],[['1','Low','Low','Average'],['2–5','Low','Average','High'],['6+','Average','High','High']])+'<p>Do not turn the five weather fields into five RETs. Fields and subgroups are different quantities.</p>',
          'A data function has 3 RETs and 25 DETs. What is its complexity?', ['Average','Low','High'], 'Three RETs selects 2–5; twenty-five DETs selects 20–50. The intersection is Average.'),
        step('Multiply counts by weights to get UFP', '<b>UFP</b> is the sum of each functional-unit count multiplied by its complexity weight.', anim('fp'),
          '<p>Ranking tells you which weight to use. It is not itself the function-point total.</p>'+table(['Unit','Low','Average','High'],[['EI',3,4,6],['EO',4,5,7],['EQ',3,4,6],['ILF',7,10,15],['EIF',5,7,10]])+'<ol><li>Weather inputs: 3 low EI × 3 = 9.</li><li>Output and inquiry: 1 × 4 + 1 × 3 = 7.</li><li>Data: 1 low ILF × 7 = 7; EIF contributes zero.</li><li>Add the contributions: 9 + 7 + 7 = <b>23 UFP</b>.</li></ol><p>Keep count, complexity and weight visible in your working. That makes a mistaken multiplier easier to find.</p>',
          'If a second low EO were added to the weather app, with everything else unchanged, what would UFP become?', ['27','24','46'], 'A low EO contributes 4 FP, so 23 + 4 = 27.'),
        step('Adjust UFP using the GSC total', 'In these slides, <b>AFP = UFP × (0.65 + 0.01 × VAF)</b>, where VAF is the GSC weight sum.', anim('fp'),
          '<p>The weather app needs more than a raw functionality count. The slides assign general system characteristics weights from zero (none) to five (essential).</p><ol><li>Sum the assigned GSC weights: weather has VAF = 40.</li><li>Calculate the multiplier: 0.65 + 0.01 × 40 = 1.05.</li><li>Multiply: 23 × 1.05 = 24.15 AFP, rounded to about 24.</li></ol><p>Significant means 4 and average means 3 on the contribution scale. These values are separate from the complexity weights used for UFP.</p>'+warn('The chapter calls the GSC sum VAF. In its formula, 40 is the sum and 1.05 is the multiplier; do not multiply UFP directly by 40.'),
          'With UFP 100 and a GSC sum of 25, what is AFP?', ['90','2500','125'], 'The multiplier is 0.65 + 0.25 = 0.90; 100 × 0.90 = 90.',
          lab('ch4/fp.html','Load the weather example: check counts 3, 1, 1, 1, 0.','Confirm low rankings, UFP 23, GSC sum 40 and AFP 24.15.')),
        step('The large worked example reaches about 500 FP', 'Following the slide’s worked sum gives <b>556 UFP</b> and <b>500.4 AFP</b>.',
          flow('Worked example adjustment', [['UFP contributions','77 + 58 + 60 + 145 + 216'],['UFP','556'],['GSC total','25 → multiplier 0.90'],['AFP','500.4 ≈ 500']]),
          '<p>The web-enabled example has several complexity groups within each unit type. Calculate a subtotal for each type before adding them.</p><ol><li>EI: 15×3 + 8×4 = 77; EO: 7×4 + 6×5 = 58.</li><li>ILF: 3×10 + 2×15 = 60; EIF: 5×7 + 11×10 = 145.</li><li>The worked EQ sum is 30×3 + 15×4 + 11×6 = 216.</li><li>Total 556; GSC sum 3+4+5+4+4+5 = 25; AFP = 556×0.90 = 500.4.</li></ol><p>The later estimation uses rounded 500 FP. By size, below 500 is simple, 500–999 average and at least 1000 complex. The slides also classify web applications as average.</p>'+warn('Slide erratum: the problem text says 7 complex inquiries, while the worked sum uses 11. Using 7 gives UFP 532; the published UFP 556 and later estimates follow 11. Keep that assumption explicit.'),
          'Why does the lesson use 11 complex inquiries when reproducing the slide’s 556 UFP?', ['The worked sum uses 11 despite the conflicting problem text','Seven and eleven produce the same total','All inquiries always have count eleven'], 'The source is inconsistent. Eleven reproduces its worked answer; seven would lower UFP by 24.'),
        step('Engineering rules separate duration, staff and effort', '<b>Duration = FP^A</b>, <b>Staff = FP/150</b>, and <b>Effort = Staff × Duration</b>.',
          flow('500-FP rules estimate', [['Average project','A = 0.40'],['Duration','500^0.40 = 12.01 months'],['Staff','500/150 = 3.33'],['Effort','3.33 × 12.01 ≈ 40 person-months']]),
          '<p>Time on a calendar and total human work have different units. Duration is months; effort is person-months.</p><p>The rules use exponent A = 0.32 for simple projects, 0.40 for average and 0.45 for complex or embedded projects.</p><ol><li>Choose average for the 500-FP web application.</li><li>Calculate duration 500^0.40 = 12.01 months.</li><li>Calculate staff 500/150 = 3.33 people.</li><li>Multiply staff and duration to obtain about 40 person-months.</li></ol>'+warn('Forty person-months does not mean a forty-month project. It is an effort estimate, not elapsed duration.'),
          'Which unit belongs on the result 3.33 × 12.01 ≈ 40?', ['Person-months','Months only','Function points'], 'People multiplied by months gives person-months of effort.'),
        step('Convert FP to KLOC before applying COCOMO I', '<b>COCOMO I</b> uses KLOC and class-specific coefficients; the example combines its effort with the rules estimate.', anim('estimate'),
          '<p>The same 500 FP will be built with Java (60%), PL/SQL (25%) and Perl (15%). Each language has a different LOC-per-FP factor in the example.</p><ol><li>LOC = 500×0.60×53 + 500×0.25×37 + 500×0.15×24 = 22,325, or about 22.3 KLOC.</li><li>Use E = a×KLOC^b and D = c×E^d with the appropriate class below.</li><li>Semi-detached: E = 3×22.3^1.12 ≈ 97.1 person-months; D = 2.5×97.1^0.35 ≈ 12.4 months.</li><li>Weighted effort = (7×40 + 3×97)/10 = 57.1 ≈ 57 person-months.</li></ol>'+table(['Class','Situation','a','b','c','d'],[['Organic','Small, known environment, experienced team',2.4,1.05,2.5,0.38],['Semi-detached','Medium; web example',3,1.12,2.5,0.35],['Embedded','Tight hardware/software constraints',3.6,1.20,2.5,0.32]])+'<p>The slides also name COCOMO II. This numerical exercise uses the supplied COCOMO I relations.</p>'+warn('Do not feed 22,325 LOC into a KLOC formula. Organic, semi-detached and embedded select different coefficients; embedded describes tight constraints, not simply a large team.'),
          'Why divide the weighted effort sum by 10?', ['The weights 7 and 3 sum to 10','There are ten developers','KLOC always divides effort by ten'], 'This is a weighted average: divide by the sum of the weights.',
          lab('ch4/estimate.html','Use 500 FP with average complexity to obtain about 40 person-months.','Use 22.3 KLOC and semi-detached COCOMO I; compare about 97 person-months with about 12.4 months.','Apply weights 7 and 3 to check the combined effort near 57 person-months.')),
        step('Agree on priorities before breaking work into tasks', '<b>Expectations management</b> sets priorities; a <b>WBS</b> breaks the project into manageable work.',
          flow('From priorities to schedule', [['Priorities','Cost, schedule, scope/quality'],['WBS','Goal → phases → activities → tasks'],['PERT','Task dependencies'],['Gantt','Calendar bars and progress']]),
          '<p>The lunar example allows the budget to rise, but makes the deadline especially important. Its matrix uses Cost = Accept, Schedule = Constrain and Scope/Quality = Max or Min.</p><p>Each measure gets one X, and each matrix column is used once. The matrix makes the tradeoff explicit before a conflict occurs.</p><p>A WBS decomposes work, as in the “1.0 Tree house” example. The chapter limits a task to 40 working hours and at most six subtasks.</p><p>A PERT network shows interdependencies. A Gantt chart puts tasks against a calendar, making overlap and percentage completion visible.</p>',
          'Which view would best show whether two tasks overlap on the calendar?', ['A Gantt chart','A list of FP weights','The GSC contribution scale'], 'The slides describe Gantt bars against a calendar, where overlapping tasks are clear.'),
        step('Read dependencies before calculating the network', '<b>Predecessors</b> determine which tasks must finish before another can proceed.',
          {type:'graph',w:720,h:280,nodes:{a:[45,140,'1: 5'],b:[190,65,'2: 4'],c:[190,215,'3: 6'],d:[335,140,'4: 2'],e:[475,65,'5: 5.5'],f:[475,215,'6: 5'],g:[590,215,'7: 3'],h:[675,140,'8: 1']},edges:[['a','b'],['a','c'],['b','d'],['c','d'],['d','e'],['d','f'],['f','g'],['e','h'],['g','h']],cap:'Labels are task number: duration in weeks. Both 2 and 3 precede 4; both 5 and 7 precede 8.'},
          '<p>The eight-task example starts with task 1, lasting five weeks. Tasks 2 and 3 can then branch off, but task 4 needs both of them.</p>'+table(['Task','Duration','Predecessors'],[[1,5,'Start'],[2,4,'1'],[3,6,'1'],[4,2,'2, 3'],[5,5.5,'4'],[6,5,'4'],[7,3,'6'],[8,1,'5, 7']])+'<p>Task 8 must wait for both task 5 and task 7. A branch reaching it early cannot make the project finish by itself.</p><p>The critical path will be the path with the largest total duration. First compute the completion times instead of guessing from the number of tasks.</p>',
          'Task 5 finishes but task 7 is still running. Can task 8 begin?', ['No, it needs both predecessors','Yes, any one predecessor is enough','Yes, because task 5 has a decimal duration'], 'Both incoming dependencies must be satisfied before task 8 starts.'),
        step('The forward pass waits for the latest predecessor', '<b>TE = max(predecessor TE) + own duration</b> gives earliest completion.', anim('critical'),
          '<p>Move from task 1 toward task 8. TE means earliest completion, not earliest start.</p><ol><li>TE1 = 5; TE2 = 5+4 = 9; TE3 = 5+6 = 11.</li><li>Task 4 waits for both branches: TE4 = max(9,11)+2 = 13.</li><li>TE5 = 13+5.5 = 18.5; TE6 = 13+5 = 18; TE7 = 18+3 = 21.</li><li>TE8 = max(18.5,21)+1 = <b>22 weeks</b>.</li></ol><p>The maximum is necessary because an unfinished predecessor still blocks the next task.</p>'+warn('Do not add all branch completion times together. At a merge, choose the largest predecessor TE, then add the current duration.'),
          'Why is TE4 equal to 13 rather than 11?', ['It waits until week 11, then needs its own 2 weeks','It takes the shortest predecessor plus 2','It adds the two predecessor durations only'], 'Task 3 finishes at 11; task 4 cannot finish until its own two weeks have elapsed.'),
        step('The backward pass finds slack and the critical path', '<b>TL = min(successor TL − successor duration)</b>; <b>slack = TL − TE</b>.', anim('critical'),
          '<p>Start from the project finish, TL8 = 22. Work backward to find how late each task may finish without delaying that endpoint.</p><ol><li>TL7 = 22−1 = 21; TL5 = 22−1 = 21; TL6 = 21−3 = 18.</li><li>TL4 = min(21−5.5,18−5) = 13.</li><li>TL2 = TL3 = 13−2 = 11; TL1 = min(11−4,11−6) = 5.</li><li>Subtract TE from TL to get slack for each task.</li></ol>'+table(['Task','TE','TL','Slack'],[[1,5,5,0],[2,9,11,2],[3,11,11,0],[4,13,13,0],[5,18.5,21,2.5],[6,18,18,0],[7,21,21,0],[8,22,22,0]])+'<p>The zero-slack chain is <b>1–3–4–6–7–8</b>. Its duration is 22 weeks; delaying a critical task delays the project.</p>'+warn('Backward processing subtracts the successor’s duration from its TL, then takes the minimum across successors. Task 5 has slack 2.5, not zero.'),
          'Task 5 is delayed by two weeks while all other timings stay the same. What happens to project completion?', ['It can still finish at week 22','It must finish at week 24','The critical path changes to every task'], 'Task 5 has 2.5 weeks of slack, so a two-week delay fits within its allowance.',
          lab('ch4/critical.html','Load Slide network and step through the forward pass to TE8 = 22.','Complete the backward pass and find task 2 slack = 2 and task 5 slack = 2.5.','Trace 1–3–4–6–7–8 and add its durations to verify 22 weeks.'))
      ]
    },
    5: {
      goal: 'write and classify requirements, investigate causes with a fishbone diagram, choose fact-finding methods, and calculate a systematic sample.', time:'80–100 min',
      steps: [
        step('Write down the need before choosing the solution', 'A <b>requirement</b> states something the system must do or a property it must have.',
          flow('From need to agreement', [['Business','High-level goals'],['Stakeholders','What users expect'],['Solution','Functional and nonfunctional requirements'],['SRS','Written agreement']]),
          '<p>A student wants to see a weekly schedule. Writing that need down helps the team agree on what must be delivered.</p><p>Business requirements describe high-level goals. Stakeholder requirements explain expectations, and solution requirements specify product characteristics that meet those needs.</p><p>Requirements analysis creates the SRS with owners, managers, analysts and users involved. Writing completes understanding and forms a contract between customer and developer.</p>'+videos([['requirements','Requirements'],['factfinding','Fact-finding']]),
          'Why write requirements instead of relying only on a conversation?', ['To complete understanding and establish an agreement','To choose every programming algorithm immediately','To make user participation unnecessary'], 'The chapter gives understanding and a developer–customer contract as reasons for writing requirements.'),
        step('Separate the service from the conditions for a satisfactory service', '<b>Functional</b> requirements specify services; <b>nonfunctional</b> requirements specify qualities and constraints.', anim('requirements'),
          '<p>“Display the student’s weekly schedule” names an activity. It says what the system provides.</p><p>“Respond within six seconds under the stated workload” adds a condition for satisfactory operation. It describes performance, rather than adding another user service.</p><p>The slides also count required technology, interfaces, GUI limits and error handling as nonfunctional. Classify the statement according to this course’s examples.</p>'+warn('A requirement can be very specific and still be nonfunctional. Required Java/Eclipse technology is a constraint, not a user service.'),
          'The system must be implemented in Java using Eclipse. Why is this nonfunctional in the slides?', ['It constrains the solution technology','It is too vague to be a requirement','It names a service for students'], 'The example explicitly calls Java and Eclipse a software constraint.'),
        step('Quality requirements need a measurable condition', '<b>Quality attributes</b> describe how reliably, quickly and easily the service must work.',
          compare('Measure the intended quality', [['Reliability','At most two level-one faults per month'],['Availability','98% of monthly business hours'],['Maintainability','Repair a severity-2 defect within 8 person-hours'],['Performance','At most 6 seconds under the given workload']]),
          '<p>The dashboard being available 98% of business hours is different from it running without failures. Availability asks whether it is accessible; reliability concerns failure-free operation.</p><p>The performance example includes 5,000 users per hour, Chrome desktop rendering and an LTE connection. Removing these conditions changes what the six-second limit means.</p><p>Other examples cover security, portability, compatibility and usability. The slides use Windows/Linux for portability, specified supported versions for compatibility, and at most 10% checkout-entry errors for usability.</p>',
          'Why retain the workload and connection conditions in the six-second response requirement?', ['They define the conditions under which the limit must hold','They turn it into a new user service','They prove all future workloads are supported'], 'The timing claim is meaningful for the stated operating conditions, not every possible environment.'),
        step('Constraints and interfaces can be requirements too', '<b>Nonfunctional requirements</b> also include design limits, standards, interfaces and error handling.',
          compare('Course examples beyond qualities', [['Constraints','GPA at two-digit precision','Specified server and documentation standard'],['Interfaces','Barcode reader and finance intranet','At most four functions per menu'],['Error handling','Restrict login after three unsuccessful attempts']]),
          '<p>The slides require a particular server, company documentation guidelines and communication over the company intranet. These restrict how a satisfactory solution can be built or connected.</p><p>A GUI requirement limits each menu to four functions. Error handling specifies what happens when something goes wrong, such as restricting login access after three unsuccessful attempts.</p><p>Do not classify a statement only by the word “shall”. Ask whether it supplies a service or constrains the way the system must work.</p>',
          'Which statement supplies a functional service rather than one of the slide’s constraints?', ['Send a welcome email when an account is created','Use the specified Xeon server','Limit each menu to four functions'], 'Sending a welcome email is one of the functional examples; the other two constrain the solution.',
          lab('ch5/requirements.html','Classify weekly schedule display as functional.','Find performance, availability and software-constraint examples.','Classify login restriction as error handling, following the slides.')),
        step('A fishbone separates a visible problem from possible causes', 'A <b>fishbone diagram</b> organizes cause-and-effect hypotheses for investigation.',
          {type:'graph',w:680,h:250,nodes:{p:[580,125,'Problem'],a:[100,40,'Main cause A'],b:[100,210,'Main cause B'],c:[320,40,'Main cause C'],d:[320,210,'Main cause D']},edges:[['a','p'],['b','p'],['c','p'],['d','p']],cap:'Simplified cause map: put the problem at the right; group causes and sub-causes along the backbone.'},
          '<p>A complaint describes an effect. The analyst still needs to investigate what could be causing it, instead of assuming that the first explanation is correct.</p><ol><li>Write the problem at the head on the right.</li><li>Draw a backbone and four to eight main cause branches.</li><li>Add causes and sub-causes, using brainstorming to generate possibilities.</li></ol><p>The diagram makes possible causes visible together. Requirements discovery then gathers evidence about the system and its users.</p><p>The diagram is a tool for analysis; a branch label alone does not prove a root cause.</p>',
          'Several possible causes are listed on a fishbone. What is the analyst’s next responsibility?', ['Investigate them rather than treat every label as proven','Implement the first cause as a requirement immediately','Delete all sub-causes'], 'The diagram organizes possible causes to support analysis and root-cause discovery.'),
        step('Choose a fact-finding method for the missing evidence', '<b>Research, observation and prototyping</b> answer different questions about the work.', anim('facts'),
          '<p>If you need to learn how similar projects solved a problem, research their reference materials. If you need to see actual work, watch or participate in that work.</p>'+table(['Method','Useful evidence','Limitation'],[['Research','Solutions in similar projects and references','Must still understand this system’s needs'],['Observation','What people actually do in complex tasks','People may behave differently when watched'],['Prototyping','User feedback on a small working model','Can raise unrealistic expectations or extend the schedule']])+'<p>A prototype lets users try an idea before full development. It helps check usefulness and feasibility.</p><p>Choose the method from the information gap, rather than using the same technique for every question.</p>',
          'Users struggle to explain a complex task, and you need to see the actual steps. Which approach fits best?', ['Observe the work while allowing for changed behavior under observation','Count only source-code lines','Treat a prototype as the finished product'], 'Observation exposes actual actions, but the observer must remember that being watched can affect behavior.',
          lab('ch5/facts.html','Match actual work with observation and similar-project evidence with research.','Match a small working model with prototyping; state its expectation risk.')),
        step('Sampling collects evidence without reading every record', '<b>Random, systematic and stratified sampling</b> choose records in different ways.',
          compare('Three sampling choices', [['Random','Each member has equal chance','Rare characteristics may be missed'],['Systematic','Regular intervals','Source patterns can create bias'],['Stratified','Split into relevant subgroups first','Ensure subgroup representation']]),
          '<p>Completed forms, records and existing screens can reveal how the current system works. Sampling gathers a representative selection of that evidence.</p><p>Random selection gives each member an equal chance, but can miss enough examples of an uncommon characteristic. Systematic selection uses a regular interval.</p><p>Stratified sampling first divides the population into groups, then samples within them. The slides suggest grouping past software projects by application type to represent different complexities.</p>',
          'You need examples from every application type among past projects. Which approach directly supports that aim?', ['Stratify by application type before sampling','Take every record from only the largest type','Assume a random sample always contains every rare type'], 'Stratification is used to ensure representation of the relevant subgroups.'),
        step('Systematic sampling uses population divided by sample size', '<b>Interval = x/n</b>: select every tenth record when x = 1000 and n = 100.', anim('sampling'),
          '<p>The slide’s analyst wants 100 records from a population of 1,000. The interval tells us how far apart the selected records are.</p><ol><li>Identify population x = 1000 and desired sample n = 100.</li><li>Divide x/n = 10, so select every tenth record.</li><li>For the invoice example, 6800/68 = 100: select every hundredth invoice.</li></ol><p>Slide 43 estimates sample size as 0.25 × (certainty factor / acceptable error)^2. Its certainty factors are 1.960 for 95%, 1.645 for 90% and 1.281 for 80%.</p><p>At 90% with error 0.10: 0.25×(1.645/0.10)^2 = 67.65, about 68 records. Regular patterns in the population can still bias a systematic sample.</p>',
          'For a population of 6800 and sample of 68, what selection interval follows the slide rule?', ['100','68','10'], '6800 divided by 68 is 100, so choose every hundredth record.',
          lab('ch5/sampling.html','Set population 1000 and sample 100; inspect the interval 10.','Set population 6800 and sample 68; confirm interval 100.','Use certainty 90% and error 0.10 to reproduce approximately 68 records.')),
        step('Questionnaires trade reach for limited follow-up', '<b>Questionnaires</b> gather many responses cheaply, but unclear questions cannot easily be explained afterward.',
          compare('Question format shapes the answer', [['Open-ended','Explain how you book a flight'],['Closed-ended','Yes/no or multiple choice'],['Frequency / agreement','Never to Always: Likert in these slides','Strongly agree to Strongly disagree: rating']]),
          '<p>The flight-booking question lets respondents describe their process freely. A question with preset options restricts the answer but is easier to tabulate.</p><p>The slides distinguish frequency Likert questions from agreement rating questions. A ranking question asks respondents to prioritize transactions by time spent.</p><p>Determine what facts are needed, choose respondents and formats, write the questions, then test on a small sample. Questionnaires offer convenience and anonymity but often have low returns or incomplete answers.</p>',
          'Why pilot a questionnaire on a small sample first?', ['To discover unclear questions before distributing it widely','To guarantee every recipient will reply','To eliminate the need to choose respondents'], 'The development steps include a small test; this matters because there is little opportunity to explain misunderstood questions later.'),
        step('Interviews probe answers; JRP aligns people in one session', '<b>Interviews</b> allow follow-up; <b>JRP</b> brings users, management and IT together to define requirements.',
          compare('Who does what in JRP?', [['Facilitator','Plans, conducts and follows through'],['Sponsor','Top management; leads with facilitator'],['Users and managers','Explain goals, priorities and needs'],['IT staff','Listen and take notes']]),
          '<p>An interview lets the analyst reword a question or probe an unexpected answer. Structured interviews use prepared questions; unstructured interviews begin with a general goal.</p><p>Use clear, short questions and avoid inserting your own preferred answer. Interviews take time and depend strongly on the analyst’s interpersonal skills.</p><p>JRP is a joint planning workshop. Select participants, prepare an agenda and use a suitable location away from the workplace. The result is written requirements, with users and managers actively involved.</p>',
          'Which interview question best avoids leading the user?', ['What information do you need in this report?','You will not use that operator code, will you?','Twenty codes should be enough, correct?'], 'The first asks for the user’s information need without supplying the interviewer’s opinion.')
      ]
    },
    6: {
      goal: 'describe actor goals and scenarios, draw include and extend in the right direction, write testable stories, and trace choices and parallel flows in an activity diagram.', time:'100–120 min',
      steps: [
        step('A use case is a meaningful goal at the system boundary', 'A <b>use case</b> describes actor–system interactions that complete one business task.',
          compare('Name the goal and the participant', [['Use case','Verb phrase','Create order; display weekly schedule'],['Actor','Noun phrase','External person, system or device']]),
          '<p>The slide’s shopper wants to place an order. That goal makes sense to a user, even without knowing the program’s internal functions.</p><p>A use case describes the interactions needed to complete such meaningful work. Name it with a verb phrase, such as “Create order”.</p><p>An actor exchanges information with the system from outside its boundary. An actor can be a person, organization, external device or another system.</p>'+videos([['usecase','Use case modeling'],['userstory','User stories'],['activity','Activity diagrams']]),
          'Which name best expresses a user-level use case?', ['Place order','Order database table','Internal loop counter'], 'Place order names meaningful business work, rather than an internal implementation part.'),
        step('The primary actor depends on whose system you are modeling', 'A <b>primary actor</b> pursues the goal; a <b>supporting actor</b> provides a needed external service.',
          compare('Two slide examples', [['Process Sale at POS','Primary: cashier','Supporting: credit bureau'],['Enterprise online shopping','Primary: customer','Supporting: credit bureau']]),
          '<p>At the checkout, the cashier uses the POS system to record purchases. The slides therefore identify the cashier as the primary actor of Process Sale.</p><p>The credit bureau authorizes a card charge and supplies a service, so it is supporting. For online shopping, the customer directly uses the web system and becomes primary.</p><p>Roles depend on the boundary and use case. A participant can be primary in one case and supporting in another.</p>'+warn('Do not assume that the customer is always the primary actor. In the slide’s POS example it is the cashier.'),
          'In Process Sale, why is the credit bureau a supporting actor?', ['It supplies authorization needed for the sale','It is an internal POS module','It is the person recording every purchased item'], 'The bureau is external and provides a service that helps the primary actor complete the goal.'),
        step('A scenario follows an order from its starting conditions to its outcome', 'A fully dressed <b>scenario</b> records conditions and <b>normal and alternate flows</b>.',
          flow('Place Order conditions', [['Before','Registered shopper; items selected and in stock'],['Trigger','Shopper clicks Place Order'],['After','Order placed; tracking ID and delivery date known']]),
          '<p>The Place Order scenario begins when the shopper indicates a desire to buy the selected items. The click is the trigger.</p><p>Preconditions describe what must already hold: registered user, selected items and stock availability. Postconditions describe the successful outcome: placed order, tracking ID and estimated delivery date.</p><p>The normal flow authenticates the shopper, presents charges and confirms details. It then uses billing, shipping, bank and fulfillment services.</p><p>Alternate flows cover invalid credentials, changed items and card problems. State the condition and where processing returns or ends. An invalid password causes an error and another request for credentials.</p><p>The scenario also identifies primary and supporting actors, a description and business rules. These sections make it possible to discuss the same event from several perspectives without losing the main goal.</p>'+warn('The written business rule says billing occurs only when products ship, but the normal-flow text says the bank charges earlier. This example needs clarification; do not silently invent a consistent payment rule.'),
          'Where should “the shopper has selected items” appear in the supplied Place Order scenario?', ['Preconditions','Only postconditions','The system’s implementation language'], 'Selected items are required before this use case proceeds.'),
        step('A use case diagram summarizes who can do what', '<b>Associations</b> link external actors to use cases inside the system boundary.',
          compare('Read the diagram’s shapes', [['Rectangle','System boundary'],['Oval','A use case / user goal'],['Actor and solid line','External participant associated with a goal']]),
          '<p>The scenario contains detailed interaction steps. The diagram provides a shorter view of the actors and goals.</p><p>Draw the boundary first so external actors are distinguishable from internal behavior. Put use cases inside it and associate participants with their goals.</p><p>An actor must be associated with at least one use case. One actor can use several goals, and a goal can involve several actors.</p>',
          'Does a plain association line tell you that one use case always invokes another?', ['No, it connects an actor with a use case','Yes, every line means include','Yes, every actor must be inside the boundary'], 'Association is actor–use case participation. Include and extend describe different relationships.'),
        step('Include always calls the behavior at the arrow tip', '<b>Include</b> points from the base use case to the included use case, whose behavior is always called.',
          {type:'graph',w:680,h:240,nodes:{b:[130,120,'Borrow Books'],f:[490,55,'Check Fine'],r:[490,185,'Check Reserved Book']},edges:[['b','f','include'],['b','r','include']],cap:'Direction sketch of the slide: both include arrows start at Borrow Books. UML uses dotted lines for these arrows.'},
          '<p>The Borrow Books diagram reuses Check Fine and Check Reserved Book. Both behaviors are included in borrowing.</p><p>Follow the arrow from Borrow Books to each included behavior. The dotted line is labeled include, with its arrowhead at the child behavior.</p><p>This relationship means the included behavior is always called when the base runs. It is useful for behavior shared by use cases.</p>'+warn('Include direction is <b>base → included</b>. Reversing it changes who calls whom; include is not optional behavior.'),
          'Borrow Books includes Check Fine. Where does the include arrow point?', ['From Borrow Books to Check Fine','From Check Fine to Borrow Books','From the actor to the boundary'], 'The base calls the included behavior at the arrow tip.'),
        step('Generalization points from the specialized child to its parent', '<b>Generalization</b> uses a hollow triangle pointing to the more general actor or use case.',
          compare('Read inheritance', [['Child at arrow source','Specialized actor or use case','Can add its own behavior'],['Parent at hollow triangle','The general form','Common inherited functions']]),
          '<p>Two actor types can share many interactions while each also has unique work. The slides use generalization to express that shared inheritance.</p><p>The same parent–child idea can apply to use cases. A specialized child enhances the parent rather than merely calling a mandatory shared task.</p><p>In UML, the line is solid and the arrowhead is a hollow triangle. Read it from child toward parent.</p>',
          'A hollow triangle touches the parent use case. Which relationship is being shown?', ['Generalization from child to parent','Include from base to child','Extend from base to extension'], 'The hollow triangle and parentward direction distinguish generalization from dotted include and extend arrows.'),
        step('Extend adds conditional behavior to an independent base', '<b>Extend</b> points from the optional extension to the base use case.', anim('usecase'),
          '<p>Deposit Funds is meaningful on its own. Calculate Bonus adds behavior when the extension rule holds.</p><p>The slide’s condition is a deposit over 10,000 or age over 55. The extension therefore does not have to run on every deposit.</p><p>The dotted extend arrow starts at Calculate Bonus and points to Deposit Funds. This direction is opposite to the base-to-child reading used for include.</p>'+warn('An extension depends on its base; the base must remain meaningful without it. <b>Extension → base</b> is the extend direction.'),
          'A deposit is 9000 and the person is 40. What follows from the stated extension rule?', ['Deposit Funds can complete without Calculate Bonus','The base use case becomes meaningless','Include must run because every deposit earns the extension'], 'Neither condition holds, so the optional bonus behavior is not invoked while the base remains meaningful.',
          lab('ch6/usecase.html','Choose include and set the direction base → included.','Choose extend and reverse the roles: extension → base.','Use the questions to identify the cashier and credit bureau in Process Sale.')),
        step('A user story connects a role, capability and benefit', 'A <b>user story</b> describes a feature from the end user’s perspective and supports conversation.',
          flow('The admin story', [['Role','As an admin'],['Capability','I want to add product descriptions'],['Benefit','So users can compare products']]),
          '<p>The slide’s admin wants to add product descriptions so users can view and compare them. The benefit explains why the capability matters.</p><p>The common template is “As a role, I can capability, so that benefit.” Stories belong in an ordered backlog and are supported by conversations about the desired functionality.</p><p>The slides organize larger work as initiatives, then epics, then stories. The video-player initiative contains an improved streaming epic and smaller stories such as a desktop fullscreen button.</p>',
          'Why keep the benefit in the product-description story?', ['It explains the user value behind the requested capability','It dictates the exact database design','It replaces all acceptance criteria'], 'The benefit connects the action to a reason the user needs it.'),
        step('INVEST and acceptance criteria make a story workable', '<b>INVEST</b> checks story quality; <b>acceptance criteria</b> make completion observable.', anim('invest'),
          '<p>“Make everything better” gives the team little basis for planning or checking success. A useful story has a clear scope and a testable result.</p>'+table(['Letter','Ask'],[['I: Independent','Can it be developed without another story changing it?'],['N: Negotiable','Can implementation details be discussed?'],['V: Valuable','Does it deliver user value?'],['E: Estimable','Is enough known to estimate?'],['S: Small','Can it fit through design, code and testing in a sprint?'],['T: Testable','Are there clear acceptance criteria?']])+'<p>The auction example requires the bidder to log in, navigate to the auction page and select products to bid on. These are observable criteria, rather than a feeling that the system is good.</p><p>A story is small enough for a sprint; an epic can span several sprints. Split large work before committing to one iteration.</p>',
          'A story has a role and benefit but says “done when it feels easy”. Which improvement is most needed?', ['Observable acceptance criteria','A larger epic with more features','Removal of the user role'], 'Testable stories need clear criteria that establish whether the required behavior is implemented.',
          lab('ch6/invest.html','Review the product-description story and its acceptance criteria.','For each INVEST tick, identify evidence in the text.','Load the vague story and identify missing scope and testable criteria.')),
        step('Design models answer different questions about the same system', '<b>Structural UML</b> shows organization; <b>behavioral UML</b> shows what happens.',
          compare('Choose a view for the question', [['Structural','Class, object, component, deployment','Package, profile, composite structure'],['Behavioral','Use case, activity, sequence, state machine','Communication, interaction overview, timing']]),
          '<p>A use case describes required work, but builders also need to understand internal structure. Software design develops architecture and detailed decisions for that purpose.</p><p>UML is independent of implementation language. The chapter groups its fourteen diagrams into seven structural and seven behavioral diagrams.</p><p>Choose the view for the question: classes and components show structure, while activities and message exchanges show behavior. A diagram is a way to communicate a part of the system, not the entire design at once.</p>',
          'You need to show the flow of a business process. Which UML family fits?', ['Behavioral, using an activity diagram','Structural, using only a deployment view','Neither, because UML depends on the programming language'], 'Activity diagrams are behavioral views of control or object flow.'),
        step('Activity actions and lanes show work and responsibility', 'An <b>action</b> is an atomic step; a <b>partition</b> groups actions by a shared responsibility.',
          compare('Activity notation', [['Rounded rectangle','Action: one atomic step','Usually named with an action verb'],['Concave pentagon','Accept event action','Wait for a specified event'],['Partition / swimlane','Group related actions','Often an actor or organizational unit']]),
          '<p>Break the business process into actions that are not further decomposed in this activity. Give each rounded rectangle an action name.</p><p>An accept event action waits for an event, rather than immediately performing ordinary work. Its shape is a concave pentagon.</p><p>Partitions group actions, often by who performs them. Edges connect nodes and can carry control tokens or data objects; an object-flow edge shows data moving between actions.</p>',
          'Why add partitions to a process with several organizational roles?', ['To show which responsibility groups perform the actions','To force every action to run in parallel','To replace all action names with actors'], 'Partitions group actions by a common characteristic, often a responsible actor or unit.'),
        step('A choice rejoins with a merge; parallel work synchronizes with a join', '<b>Decision/merge</b> handles alternatives; <b>fork/join</b> handles concurrent flows.',
          compare('Two different splits', [['Decision → merge','Diamond: select one guarded path','Diamond: bring alternatives together'],['Fork → join','Bar: start concurrent flows','Bar: wait for concurrent arrivals']]),
          '<p>When a condition chooses one path, the other alternative does not run. A decision diamond selects the outgoing path, and a merge brings alternatives back together.</p><p>A fork starts multiple concurrent flows. A join synchronizes their arrivals before work continues.</p><p>Look at both the shape and its incoming and outgoing edges. A split and a reunion do not always represent the same kind of coordination.</p>'+warn('A merge does not wait for all parallel branches. Use fork/join for parallel work and decision/merge for a choice.'),
          'Two independent activities must both finish before the next action. What should combine them?', ['A join','A merge of alternatives','A decision that chooses only one'], 'A join synchronizes concurrent incoming flows; a merge combines alternative paths.'),
        step('An activity final stops everything; a flow final stops one flow', '<b>Activity final</b> ends all activity flows; <b>flow final</b> ends only the arriving flow.',
          compare('Three circle symbols', [['Filled circle','Initial node','Flow starts'],['Bullseye','Activity final','All flows stop'],['Circle with X','Flow final','Only this flow stops']]),
          '<p>Imagine two branches are active at once. One branch finishing does not always mean the whole activity should end.</p><p>A flow final destroys tokens arriving at that node and leaves other flows alone. An activity final stops every flow as soon as it is reached.</p><p>An activity may contain more than one activity final. The first reached still stops all flows, rather than waiting for the others.</p>'+warn('The bullseye and the circle with X are not interchangeable: all flows versus one flow is the exam distinction.'),
          'One parallel branch reaches a flow final while another branch still has work. What happens?', ['The other branch can continue','All activity flows must stop','The initial node automatically restarts'], 'Only tokens arriving at a flow final are destroyed; other flows are unaffected.')
      ]
    },
    7: {
      goal: 'trace activity and sequence examples, select interaction fragments, follow ATM state transitions, and read classes and component interface connections.', time:'100–120 min',
      steps: [
        step('Trace online shopping as actions and choices', '<b>Activity diagrams</b> show the order and conditions of actions, rather than messages between participants.',
          flow('Online-shopping action summary', [['Browse or search','Find an item'],['View item','Inspect details'],['Cart','Add, view or update'],['Checkout','Includes registration and login']]),
          '<p>The online-shopping example lets a customer browse or search, view an item, add it to a cart and check out. The customer can view the cart at any time.</p><p>The figure summarizes those actions; the full behavior includes choices and returns, rather than one forced pass through every action.</p><p>The slides assume registration and login are included in checkout. They omit partitions in this example, with most actions attributed to the online customer.</p>'+videos([['sequence','Sequence diagrams'],['state','State machines'],['structure','Structural diagrams']]),
          'Why should the cart not be interpreted as reachable only once in a strict straight line?', ['The example allows viewing the cart at any time','All shopping actions must be concurrent','The cart is an actor outside the system'], 'The written example explicitly permits cart access at any time; the action summary is not the full control-flow diagram.'),
        step('Process Order allows shipping and payment work to overlap', 'A <b>fork</b> creates concurrent flows and a <b>join</b> waits for both paths.', anim('activity'),
          '<p>The Process Order slide accepts the order and fills in required information. Shipping can proceed while the other branch sends an invoice and accepts payment.</p><p>Step the animation until shipping reaches the join. It must wait while the payment branch finishes.</p><p>This means the business flow permits shipment before invoice or payment confirmation. A model shows what the specified process allows, even if you expected a different business rule.</p>'+warn('Do not read the fork as a choice between shipping and payment. Both branches run, and the join synchronizes them.'),
          'Shipping is complete but payment has not reached the join. Can the joined outgoing flow proceed?', ['No, the join waits for the concurrent payment path','Yes, the first arriving branch always wins','Only if the join is renamed a fork'], 'A join synchronizes concurrent paths; it is not a merge for alternatives.',
          lab('ch7/activity.html','Identify a fork and a join from their bars and edge directions.','Distinguish a decision from a merge by the alternative flows.','Answer the activity-final and flow-final items using all flows versus one flow.')),
        step('Swimlanes add responsibility to the action flow', '<b>Partitions</b> show who performs grouped actions; they do not create parallelism by themselves.',
          compare('Responsibilities in slide examples', [['Document process','Author, Reviewer, Approver, Owner','Create, review, update, approve, archive'],['Trial activation','Order Management, Customer Service, Customer','New key and C2V file needed before activation']]),
          '<p>The document process moves work among author, reviewer, approver and owner. Horizontal swimlanes show those responsibilities.</p><p>The trial-activation example uses vertical lanes. Order management creates a product key while the customer prepares a C2V file; customer service needs both before activation.</p><p>The SSO example instead follows redirects and an authentication response: the partner confirms identity, and Google checks the signed response before redirecting to the requested service or displaying an error.</p>',
          'Why does a diagram with swimlanes still need a fork to express concurrent work?', ['Lanes group responsibility, while a fork specifies concurrent control flow','Every lane is automatically a thread','Lanes replace all edges'], 'Responsibility grouping alone does not specify whether actions run sequentially, alternatively or concurrently.'),
        step('A sequence diagram follows messages down lifelines', 'A <b>lifeline</b> represents a participant; messages identify a sender, receiver and communication.', anim('sequence'),
          '<p>The online bookshop customer can search the catalog, view a book, add it to a cart and check out. A sequence view asks which participants exchange messages during those interactions.</p><p>A lifeline begins with a named head and continues vertically. Read message occurrences down the page to follow the exchange.</p><p>An execution or activation marks a period of behavior, sending or waiting for a reply. A destruction occurrence ends the instance represented by the lifeline.</p>',
          'What does a message arrow add that an activity action name alone does not?', ['The communication’s sender and receiver','A guarantee that all participants run in parallel','The application’s FP complexity'], 'Sequence messages describe specific communication between identified lifelines.'),
        step('Guards choose, repeat or stop a sequence', '<b>alt, opt, loop and break</b> specify different guarded interaction behaviors.',
          compare('Select the operator by behavior', [['alt','Choose between alternative operands'],['opt','Run one optional operand or nothing'],['loop','Repeat an operand'],['break','Run this scenario instead of the remaining interaction']]),
          '<p>The login example checks unsuccessful attempts. It terminates the process when the number is more than three; otherwise bank transactions may continue.</p><p>A loop represents repeated interaction. An alt chooses among alternatives; an opt runs one optional part or does nothing.</p><p>A break replaces the remainder of the enclosing interaction with its breaking scenario. The condition attached to an interaction is its guard.</p>'+warn('Chapter 7’s login text says <b>more than three</b> attempts; chapter 5’s requirement says <b>after three</b>. Keep the threshold from the example being asked about.'),
          'In the chapter 7 wording, the failed-attempt count is exactly three. Has the “more than three” break condition become true?', ['No, it becomes true above three','Yes, more than three means at least three','It depends on the implementation language'], 'Three is not greater than three. Preserve the stated comparison rather than silently changing it.'),
        step('Parallel fragments overlap work; ref reuses an interaction', '<b>par</b> permits concurrent operands; <b>ref</b> calls another interaction.',
          compare('Two sequence tools', [['par','Potentially parallel operands','Account and amount entry in the withdrawal diagram'],['ref','Reuse another interaction','Keep a large sequence diagram manageable']]),
          '<p>The withdrawal diagram places account and amount entry in parallel operands inside an optional valid-password section. The operator matters more than how the boxes happen to be positioned.</p><p>A par does not mean choosing one operand. It permits concurrent behavior across its operands.</p><p>A ref is an interaction use that calls another interaction. It helps simplify large diagrams without rewriting the called interaction’s details.</p>',
          'A large sequence diagram needs to reuse an existing interaction. Which operator expresses that directly?', ['ref','par','break'], 'The interaction use is drawn with ref; par is for parallel operands and break skips the remaining enclosing interaction.',
          lab('ch7/sequence.html','Choose alt for alternatives and opt for a single optional interaction.','Choose loop for repetition and break for aborting the remaining login.','Choose par for concurrent operands and ref for reuse.')),
        step('A state machine follows an object’s condition over time', 'A <b>state</b> holds a condition; an event can trigger a transition to another state.',
          {type:'graph',w:680,h:240,nodes:{o:[70,120,'Off'],s:[270,120,'Self Test'],i:[560,55,'Idle'],f:[560,185,'Out of Service']},edges:[['o','s','power on / startup'],['s','i','success'],['s','f','failure']],cap:'ATM startup sketch from the slide. The successful Self Test → Idle transition is triggerless.'},
          '<p>The ATM initially stays Off. Power on runs startup and moves it into Self Test.</p><p>A failed test leads to Out of Service. Success leads to Idle, where the ATM waits for customer interaction.</p><p>A simple state has no substates; a composite state contains substates. This lets one view show the overall condition while another view expands its internal behavior.</p>',
          'How does this view differ from the Process Order activity view?', ['It follows states of an object and transitions between them','It only counts functional requirements','It must list every message sender and receiver'], 'State machines focus on an object’s states; activity diagrams focus on flows of actions.'),
        step('Leaving Serving Customer always ejects the card', 'An <b>exit action</b> runs when leaving its state, whatever caused the transition.', anim('atm'),
          '<p>Inserting a card takes the Idle ATM into Serving Customer and performs the entry action readCard.</p><p>Serving Customer contains Customer Authentication, Selecting Transaction and Transaction. Cancel can leave this composite state at any time.</p><p>Its exit action is ejectCard. This runs whether the transaction completes normally or the customer cancels.</p>'+warn('Do not attach ejectCard only to successful completion. It belongs to leaving Serving Customer, including cancellation.'),
          'The customer cancels during authentication. Which behavior still runs on leaving Serving Customer?', ['ejectCard','Only readCard again','No exit action can run on cancel'], 'The composite state’s exit action runs regardless of what caused the exit.',
          lab('ch7/atm.html','Power on, succeed in Self Test and insert a card.','Cancel during authentication and confirm the ejectCard log entry.','Repeat through a successful transaction and compare the same exit action.')),
        step('Regions and history describe the inside of a composite state', '<b>Regions</b> group states and transitions; <b>shallow history</b> remembers the last active substate.',
          compare('Inside a composite state', [['Orthogonal regions','Concurrent sets of substates'],['Initial and final','Default entry and region completion'],['Shallow history','Re-enter the last active substate']]),
          '<p>A composite state may need more than one internal view. The slides use regions to show concurrent sets of substates.</p><p>An initial pseudostate points to a default starting state. A final state marks completion of the enclosing region. Fork and join pseudostates split and combine transitions.</p><p>Shallow history allows re-entry to the last active substate before the composite was left. It differs from always taking the default initial path.</p>',
          'Which mechanism returns to the last active substate instead of always using default entry?', ['Shallow history','A plain initial pseudostate','A class attribute'], 'The slide explicitly assigns remembered re-entry to shallow history.'),
        step('A class diagram records structure rather than execution order', 'A <b>class</b> has a name, attributes and operations, and relates to other classes.',
          {type:'stack',t:'Read a UML class box from top to bottom',items:[['Name','Which class is this?','c0'],['Attributes','What features does it hold?','c1'],['Operations','What can it do?','c2']],cap:'These are compartments of one class, not phases in time.'},
          '<p>While an ATM state machine follows a changing condition, a class diagram describes the static building blocks of an object-oriented solution.</p><p>Read a class box in three compartments: name at the top, attributes in the middle and operations at the bottom.</p><p>The class describes objects that share features, constraints and meaning. Relationships between classes complete the structural view; the box positions do not describe a message sequence.</p>',
          'Does placing an operation below an attribute mean the attribute executes first?', ['No, they are class compartments rather than a timeline','Yes, all structural diagrams run top to bottom','Only if the class is named ATM'], 'The compartments classify information about a class; they do not specify execution order.'),
        step('A component offers a ball and needs a socket', '<b>Provided = ball</b> and <b>required = socket</b> identify opposite interface roles.', anim('component'),
          '<p>The slide’s Order component needs item code from Product. Product supplies it, so its item-code interface is provided.</p><p>The complete circle is the provided ball. The half-circle is the required socket on the component that needs the service.</p><p>A component is a modular unit above the class level. Its encapsulated content interacts through provided and required interfaces, potentially exposed through ports.</p>'+warn('Order being the active user does not make it the provider. In this example Product provides and Order requires.'),
          'Product supplies item code to Order. Which notation belongs on Order for that service?', ['Required socket','Provided ball','Activity final'], 'Order consumes the service; Product provides it.',
          lab('ch7/component.html','Set Product to Provided ball and Order to Required socket.','Connect the interfaces and read the result.','Answer the assembly and delegation questions after the next step’s explanation.')),
        step('Assembly connects services; delegation forwards a contract inward', '<b>Assembly</b> connects provider to user; <b>delegation</b> links an external contract to its internal realization.',
          compare('WebStore connection examples', [['Assembly / service use','Shopping Cart uses Manage Orders','Search Engine uses Search Inventory'],['Delegation','Accounting exposes a contract','Orders or Customers realizes it internally']]),
          '<p>The WebStore example contains Search Engine, Shopping Cart and Authentication. Search Engine offers Product Search while requiring Search Inventory; Shopping Cart uses Manage Orders.</p><p>An assembly connector connects provided services to the components that use them. A delegation connector connects an external contract to the internal part that carries it out.</p><p>A port is an interaction point. A dependency means an element needs another for specification or implementation. The EmployeeProvider example distinguishes a component type from its USA and Europe instances used by one report generator.</p>',
          'Accounting exposes Manage Orders and forwards it to the Orders component inside. What does that link express?', ['Delegation to the internal realization','A fork creating parallel activity flows','Generalization of an actor'], 'The external contract is linked to the internal component that realizes the behavior.')
      ]
    }
  };
})();
